  "query_preview": "..\nMonday night through Thursday: High clouds begin clearing out\nMonday night as a weak shortwave exits the region. Breezy westerly\nwinds will linger into the late evening through the Cascade \ngaps with sustained speeds around 10 mph and gusts up to 20 mph.\nElsewhere, winds will be light. Heading into Tuesday, a ridge \nof high pressure re- amplifies over the region, keeping mostly clear\nskies, light winds, and a subtle warming trend in the forecast \nthrough Thursday.\n\nLooking at temperatures, afternoon highs will generally be in\nthe 70s and low 80s, around 10 to 15 degrees above\nclimatological normals for early October. Despite the warm \ndaytime temperatures, overnight/early morning lows will still be\nchilly as is typical during the autumn months. Strong \nradiational cooling under clear skies and light winds will \nsupport low temperatures dropping into the mid to upper 30s for \nthe sheltered northern valleys. Patchy fog and frost has been \nobserved in these valleys the past several mornings and will \nlikely recur each morning through Thursday. For most locations\noutside of the sheltered northern valleys, lows will be in the\n40s.\n\nFriday through Sunday: Confidence in Friday's forecast is low, \nwith significant spread in ensemble guidance. Around half of\nensemble members depict the ridge of high pressure remaining in\nplace over the Inland Northwest through the day on Friday, while\nthe other half show a trough moving into the region. By\nSaturday, 70 percent of ensembles show a troughing pattern,\nthough there is disagreement on the strength and track of the\ntrough. When the trough does arrive, expect it to bring cooler \ntemperatures, gusty winds, and chances for precipitation. We'll \ncontinue to monitor the details on when that trough will arrive,\nwhere it will track, and how strong it will be in the coming \ndays. /Fewkes",
